<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kingdom of Aragon</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Aragon</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Kingdom of Aragon was a Christian kingdom in north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberia</a> that took part in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a> against the Almoravid and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohad</a> states of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>. Through union with Catalonia it built a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a> presence. The marriage of Ferdinand of Aragon and Isabella of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> in 1469 laid the dynastic foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-empire">Spanish Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon.jpg" alt="Kingdom of Aragon" width="320" height="268" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ecelan · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Reino_de_Arag%C3%B3n.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Aragon began as a Carolingian feudal county around the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a>, which in the first half of the 9th century became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pamplona">Pamplona</a>, later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>. Its own line of counts ended without a male heir in 922. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-iii-of-navarre">Sancho III of Pamplona</a> died in 1035 his kingdom was divided in three, and his illegitimate son Ramiro received Aragon. After the death of his brother Gonzalo, who had held Sobrarbe and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ribagorza">Ribagorza</a>, Ramiro became the first de facto king of Aragon, although he never used the title. By defeating his brother García Sánchez III he secured the kingdom's independence.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Ramiro's son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sancho-ramirez">Sancho Ramírez</a>, who also inherited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a>, was the first to call himself King of the Aragonese and Pamplonese. As the kingdom expanded south at the expense of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-andalus">al-Andalus</a>, the capital moved from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jaca">Jaca</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huesca">Huesca</a> in 1096 and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zaragoza">Zaragoza</a> in 1118. After Alfonso the Battler died childless in 1134, Navarre and Aragon chose different rulers. By 1285 the southernmost lands of present-day Aragon had been taken from the Muslims.</p><p>In 1150 the marriage of Queen <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petronilla-of-aragon">Petronilla of Aragon</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramon-berenguer-iv-count-of-barcelona">Ramon Berenguer IV, Count of Barcelona</a>, created the Crown of Aragon, which took its name from the kingdom. Their son Alfonso II inherited all their lands, and the crown came to include Catalonia and later the kingdoms of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mallorca">Majorca</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sardinia">Sardinia</a>.</p><p>In the 14th century the king's power was greatly restricted by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-aragon">Union of Aragon</a>. When the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/barcelona">Barcelona</a> died out in 1410, the Aragonese secured the election in 1412 of a Castilian prince, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ferdinand-i-of-aragon">Ferdinand of Antequera</a>, over strong Catalan opposition. The marriage of Ferdinand and Isabella in 1469 was followed by the union of the crowns of Aragon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> on the death of John II in 1479. Aragon kept its own parliamentary institutions, the Corts, until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nueva-planta-decrees">Nueva Planta decrees</a> of 1707 to 1715 ended the kingdom in law. It remained an administrative unit until 1833, and Aragon became an autonomous community of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1982.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>The kingdom's three main groups were Christians, who held power and made up about 70 per cent of the population, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mudejar">Mudéjars</a>, about 15 per cent, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jews">Jews</a>, at most about 10 per cent. Mudéjars were Muslims who stayed after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reconquista">Reconquista</a>, paid a special poll tax and could keep their communities, religion and language; they worked as craftsmen and farmers and left a lasting architectural tradition. Jews governed their communities, called aljamas, and served as treasurers, doctors, diplomats and translators. Under James I they came under the king's special protection in return for higher taxes. The anti-Jewish violence of 1391 spread from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seville">Seville</a> into Aragon, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a> a notable target.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kingdom of Aragon” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Crown of <b>Aragon</b> was born of a dynastic marriage.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-aragon">Crown of Aragon · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Cortes, where representatives of the leading towns met with the clergy and nobility, voted taxes but, unlike the parliaments of the Crown of <b>Aragon</b>, rarely restrained royal legislation, and by the sixteenth century only eighteen cities held votes.</blockquote><a href="/empire/crown-of-castile">Crown of Castile · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Granada was small, mountainous and densely populated, its numbers swollen by Muslims leaving the lands conquered by Castile and <b>Aragon</b>; estimates of its population vary widely, but the capital was probably one of the larger cities of western Europe.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-granada">Emirate of Granada · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Treaties with Pisa, Genoa, Venice, Sicily and the Crown of <b>Aragon</b> allowed Christian merchants to live in their own funduqs, with consuls and chapels, and customs duties on this trade were a major source of revenue.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hafsid-sultanate">Hafsid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Through marriage the Jimena kings also gained the neighbouring county of <b>Aragon</b>, laying the foundations for later expansion across the Pyrenees.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-navarre">Kingdom of Navarre · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1297 Pope Boniface VIII granted a Regnum Sardiniae et Corsicae to James II of Aragon, partly as compensation for <b>Aragon</b>’s withdrawal from Sicily, but the grant had to be won by force.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-sardinia">Kingdom of Sardinia · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
